# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
/node_modules
/.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# testing
/coverage

# next.js
/.next/
/out/

# production
/build

# misc
.DS_Store
*.pem

# debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# env files (can opt-in for committing if needed)
.env*
!.env.example

# vercel
.vercel

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# Throwaway booklet-verification harnesses. Rendering a real booklet means
# running the app's own getBooklet and BookletDocument against a route dumped
# from production, and that only resolves node_modules from the repo root -- so
# the harness has to live here rather than in a scratch directory. It is
# deliberately not committed: each verification builds its own, and a stale one
# checked in would be trusted without being re-run. See docs/handoff.md.
/.booklet-*.ts
/.booklet-*.tsx
/.mainver*/
/.prefix/
/.fixver/
/.verify/

# The same reasoning for import-verification harnesses. `planRoster` is pure and
# takes plain values (src/lib/import/plan.ts), but a harness still has to sit at
# the repo root to resolve exceljs and the src imports. The existing pattern only
# covered booklet ones, so every import harness showed up as an untracked file
# and the temptation was to commit it -- a checked-in harness gets trusted
# without being re-run, which is exactly how a harness that had drifted six rows
# from the deployed screen went unnoticed. Any root dotfile .ts/.tsx is scratch.
/.*.ts
/.*.tsx
